   n_rows = len(matrix)\n    n_cols = None\n    for row in matrix:\n        if not isinstance(row, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"Each row must be a list\")\n        if n_cols is None:\n            n_cols = len(row)\n        elif len(row) != n_cols:\n            raise ValueError(\"Matrix rows must have same length\")\n    if n_cols == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Matrix must have at least one column\")\n    # Compute document frequencies\n    df = [0] * n_cols\n    for row in matrix:\n        for j, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val != 0:\n                df[j] += 1\n    # Compute IDF\n    idf = []\n    for j in range(n_cols):\n        idf_val = math.log(1 + n_rows / (1 + df[j]))\n        # Round to 6 decimal places\n        idf.append(round(idf_val, 6))\n    # Build weighted matrix and row degrees\n    weighted_matrix = []\n    row_degree = []\n    for i in range(n_rows):\n        weighted_row = []\n        row_sum = 0.0\n        for j in range(n_cols):\n            w = matrix[i][j] * idf[j]\n            weighted_row.append(round(w, 6))\n            row_sum += w\n        weighted_matrix.append(weighted_row)\n        row_degree.append(round(row_sum, 6))\n    return {\n        \"idf\": idf,\n        \"weighted_matrix\": weighted_matrix,\n        \"row_degree\": row_degree\n    }\n```\n\nBut note: The rounding of idf is applied when computing idf list. Then when multiplying, we multiply by rounded idf. That's fine. Alternatively, could multiply by raw idf and then round final. I'll keep as above.\n\nNow escape for JSON string: replace backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\", newlines with \\n, etc. Write the Python code as a single line with newlines escaped? Actually we can embed newlines as \\n in JSON string. The instruction says \"encoding newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we can keep newlines as \\n. Better to write the code as a multiline string in the JSON, but JSON doesn't support multiline strings. So we need to es